@sagiegurari OK, I did a bit more verbose debugging and found out that the culprit was the expire command issued by connect-redis which is invoked whenever session object touched. Since this is not a get command, this is processed in series and waits for timeout expiration. So we would need to allow parallel calls for not only get but also other commands such as expire.  Could we allow all commands to be issued in parallel if the forceParallel option is set?


set operations have always ran in parallel. never in series.
but i do wait for all servers to finish, as you do not want to do a get before you finish the set.
from the code:
var runInParallel = !getCommand && (command.indexOf('setnx') === -1);

all non get commands (except setnx) run in parallel and you don't have any option to run in series.


Could we loosen this restriction if forceParallel is set to true, or would it become unusable if we allow sets to return as soon as one client finishes?


i wouldn't recommend that.
think of following flow: you set the session with lets say auth=true. then after only first redis was done it ends and another request comes and you do get but second redis answers first and you get auth=false and throw user out. that's a problem (the reverse with the auth would be huge security issue).
so... set should return until we are done with all servers.


OK, then what about allowing parallel for expire only? This will make it usable with connect-redis, since that is the command that causes the wait (=for timeout).


